######## ⚠️ MEASUREMENT ARTEFACT — nearly reported as a false failure ########
First policing run measured 0.00 Mbps at the receiver and looked like total packet loss.
It was NOT. Diagnosis:
  - switch EGRESS counters proved it forwarded: port1.0.1 output 604,943 -> 634,956
    packets (+30,013) and +30,000,832 bytes
  - tb470 eth3 showed "otherhost 60"
CAUSE: the test frames were addressed to 02:00:00:00:00:99, which is not eth3's MAC, so
the receiving NIC HARDWARE-FILTERED them and they never reached rx_bytes. The earlier
CoS steps worked only because tcpdump had put the interface in PROMISCUOUS mode.
Remove tcpdump and the byte counters go blind - indistinguishable from real loss.
Confirmed by re-running with NO policy at all: still 0.00 Mbps, proving the policer was
never the cause. Rebuilt the pcaps addressed to the real NIC MACs; control then measured
48.68 Mbps received against 49.99 offered.
LESSON: when measuring throughput with rx_bytes, address frames to the receiving NIC's
real MAC, or keep the interface in promiscuous mode. Cross-check against the SWITCH's
own egress counters before concluding the DUT dropped anything.

######## STEPS 3-4 RESULT (38388, MASTER port2.0.1) — PASS ########
policy-map pm-qos-m / class cm-qos-m:
    police single-rate 10000 20000 40000 action drop-red
    police counters
  show policy-map -> average rate(10000 kbps) minimum burst(20000 B) maximum burst(40000 B)

  CONTROL, no policy : offered 49.99 Mbps -> received 48.68 Mbps   (path carries full rate)
  WITH 10 Mbps policer: offered 49.99 Mbps -> received  9.81 Mbps  (CIR = 10.00 Mbps)
=> Ingress traffic limited to the configured rate, within ~2% of CIR. STEPS 3-4 PASS.

